            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book successfully unifies the fragmented common interpretation of 'the Way' by tracing its consistent scriptural definition. By defining 'the way' as covenant conduct (how one walks), it seamlessly accounts for the Old Testament ethical tradition (Jeremiah 6:16), the 'two ways' of Matthew 7:14, the early Christian movement's self-designation (which Paul defends in Acts 24:14 in strict terms of keeping the law and prophets), and Jesus's Christological claim in John 14:6. The book's core identification demonstrates a profound explanatory advantage by interpreting John 14:6 not as three disconnected metaphors, but as three compounded covenantal symbols: the conduct (way), the standard (truth), and the existence (life) perfectly embodied in Christ.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Covenant conduct or manner of life (the walk).\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The translation of prosphatos as 'freshly slain' rather than 'new' in Hebrews 10:20.\",\n    \"The typological definitions of 'the truth' as the law/standard and 'the life' as covenant-existence in John 14:6.\",\n    \"The rigid distinction drawn between way (derek/conduct) and highway (mesillah/the built road).\",\n    \"Secondary symbol definitions such as the beast as a kingdom, the lion as a wicked ruler, and the tree of life as the living nation.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The Way in the New Testament refers primarily to Jesus as the exclusive means of access or route to God, and by extension functions as a proper name for the Christian movement, distinct from the Old Testament ethical usage of 'way' as moral conduct.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"An exclusive path or means of access to God and salvation.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter denies that the primary New Testament meaning of 'the way' is a manner of life or covenant conduct, asserting instead that it functions as a metaphor for access/mediation (in John) or a sociological label (in Acts).\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"John 14:6 focuses heavily on access ('no man cometh unto the Father, but by me'), suggesting 'way' means a route or means of mediation, not a pattern of conduct.\",\n    \"Acts uses 'the Way' as a proper noun for the Christian movement, which is traditionally understood to stem from Jesus being the 'way' to salvation rather than an emphasis on their specific covenant conduct.\",\n    \"The New Testament usage is often seen as a new Christological reality rather than a continuation of Old Testament moral pathways.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Unifies the Old Testament moral/covenantal usage of 'way' with the New Testament Christological title and early church designation into a single coherent symbol.\",\n    \"Provides a tightly integrated explanation for the triad in John 14:6 by linking 'way', 'truth', and 'life' to established Old Testament covenantal definitions.\",\n    \"Perfectly accounts for Paul's defense in Acts 24:14, where he explicitly defines his participation in 'the Way' as worshiping the God of his fathers and believing all things written in the law and the prophets.\",\n    \"Grounds the concept in the semantic root of derek (to tread/walk), making the symbol a natural extension of its linguistic origin.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Reads naturally into the immediate spatial and directional language of John 14:6 without requiring a typological framework.\",\n    \"Simply explains the absolute usage of 'the Way' in Acts as a sociological label without demanding that the surrounding culture understood its covenantal depth.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Jeremiah 6:16\",\n    \"Matthew 7:14\",\n    \"Acts 24:14\",\n    \"John 14:6\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
